#. type: Title #
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Work with fermionic circuits {#work-with-fermionic-circuits}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  The concepts in this guide are currently available only in the Python API. Equivalent functionality will be made available in the C API in a future release.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This guide explains how to use the "
"[`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") to implement quantum "
"algorithms directly in fermionic space, rather than mapping to qubits "
"first.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Why use fermionic circuits {#why-use-fermionic-circuits}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Consider the traditional workflow for implementing the time evolution of a "
"fermionic Hamiltonian on a qubit-based architecture:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1.  '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Define the fermionic Hamiltonian (for example, $H = \\sum_{pq} h_{pq} "
"c^\\dagger_p c_q$).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2.  '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Map it to a qubit operator (for example, by using the Jordan-Wigner "
"mapping).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3.  '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Implement the time evolution in qubit space (using Trotterization or other "
"methods).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This approach has a fundamental limitation; early mapping to qubits discards"
" problem-aware knowledge about the fermionic structure. Standard "
"optimization passes cannot exploit fermionic symmetries or allowed term "
"reorderings, resulting in suboptimal circuits with unnecessary depths and "
"gate counts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The [`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") enables a better workflow:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2.  '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Implement the time evolution directly in fermionic space using fermionic "
"gates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3.  '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Map to qubit space as part of the transpilation process.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This approach addresses the limitation by keeping problem-aware knowledge in"
" fermionic space, where optimization passes can exploit fermionic structure "
"and commutation relations to reduce circuit depth. The fermionic circuit "
"describes what computation to perform, while transpilation handles how to "
"map it to qubits, providing a cleaner separation of concerns.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Generic mode indexing {#generic-mode-indexing}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both the [`operators`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/operators#module-qiskit_fermions.operators "
"\"qiskit_fermions.operators\") module and the "
"[`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") use generic mode-based "
"indexing that makes no assumptions about the nature of the modes. A mode is "
"simply an abstract index labeling a fermionic degree of freedom, with no "
"inherent semantics. This design choice mirrors the operators module and "
"ensures maximum flexibility. For example, you can implement time evolution "
"for any operator implementing the "
"[`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait "
"\"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") protocol, regardless of its "
"mathematical representation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Build a fermionic circuit {#build-a-fermionic-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The example below implements the time evolution described in the previous "
"section. It constructs a "
"[`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") by specifying the number of "
"fermionic modes, then adds fermionic gates from the "
"[`qiskit_fermions.circuit.library`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-library#module-qiskit_fermions.circuit.library "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library\") to implement the time evolution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The example also demonstrates how to incorporate domain knowledge into an "
"operator by using [operator term grouping](grouping#grouping-explanation). "
"By assigning group indices to the Hamiltonian terms, you preserve structural"
" information that optimization passes can exploit throughout the "
"transpilation stack.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A simple \\`FermionicCircuit\\` with a single time evolution "
"gate.](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-fermions/circuit-2.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A decomposed \\`FermionicCircuit\\` with several time evolution "
"gates.](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-fermions/circuit-3.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Notice how the operator term grouping is preserved even in simple operations"
" like decomposition. This demonstrates how structural information flows "
"through the circuit stack. To understand the full transpilation to qubits, "
"refer to the [Transpiling fermionic circuits](transpilation#transpilation-"
"explanation) guide.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Keep gates apart with a barrier {#keep-gates-apart-with-a-barrier}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The optimization passes fuse neighboring gates where they can. For example, "
"[`MergeOrbitalRotations`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/transpiler-passes-merge-orbital-"
"rotations#qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.MergeOrbitalRotations "
"\"qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.MergeOrbitalRotations\") combines a run "
"of consecutive [`OrbitalRotation`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-library-orbital-"
"rotation#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.OrbitalRotation "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.OrbitalRotation\") gates into one, and "
"[`MergeSlaterDeterminantPreparation`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/transpiler-passes-merge-slater-determinant-"
"preparation#qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.MergeSlaterDeterminantPreparation"
" \"qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.MergeSlaterDeterminantPreparation\") "
"folds an [`InitializeModes`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-library-initialize-"
"modes#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.InitializeModes "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.InitializeModes\") into the rotation that "
"follows it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Use [`FermionicCircuit.barrier()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-fermionic-"
"circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.barrier "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.barrier\") when you want to "
"prevent that. A barrier carries no unitary effect, so it does not change the"
" state your circuit prepares; it only marks a point that the passes do not "
"fuse across. Call it without arguments to span every mode, or pass the modes"
" to restrict it to:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A \\`FermionicCircuit\\` with a barrier separating two orbital "
"rotations.](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-fermions/circuit-5.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Barriers survive the fermion-to-qubit transpilation, so they also constrain "
"the qubit-level optimization that follows. The [Transpile fermionic "
"circuits](transpilation#transpilation-explanation) guide covers that "
"stage.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Repeat a circuit {#repeat-a-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Many algorithms apply the same block of gates many times over. Time "
"evolution is the common case: a Trotter step is built once, then repeated to"
" reach the target time. Use "
"[`repeat()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-"
"fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.repeat "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.repeat\") to build the repeated "
"circuit, and [`compose()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-fermionic-"
"circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.compose "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.compose\") to join it to a state "
"preparation prefix.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The example below builds a second-order Trotter step for a Fermi-Hubbard "
"model. The step is symmetric: it splits the hopping term into two half-"
"duration [`OrbitalRotation`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-library-orbital-"
"rotation#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.OrbitalRotation "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.OrbitalRotation\") gates surrounding the "
"on-site interaction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Repeating the step and composing it onto a Hartree-Fock reference gives the "
"full evolution:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A \\`FermionicCircuit\\` with repeated steps.](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-8.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The repetitions are flattened into the returned circuit rather than wrapped "
"in a single gate, so "
"[`count_ops()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-"
"fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.count_ops "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.count_ops\") counts two rotations"
" per step. That flattening is what makes the redundancy at the step "
"boundaries visible to the transpiler: the trailing half-duration rotation of"
" one step and the leading one of the next are adjacent, so "
"[`MergeOrbitalRotations`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/transpiler-passes-merge-orbital-"
"rotations#qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.MergeOrbitalRotations "
"\"qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.MergeOrbitalRotations\") combines them "
"into a single rotation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A \\`FermionicCircuit\\` with repeated steps and merged orbital "
"rotations.](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-fermions/circuit-10.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The result costs one rotation per step plus one, which is what you get by "
"fusing those half-steps by hand. Writing the step in its natural symmetric "
"form and repeating it produces the same circuit, so the fusion is left to "
"the optimization stage.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"When you need the repetitions kept apart, pass `insert_barriers=True` to "
"place a [`barrier()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-fermionic-"
"circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.barrier "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.barrier\") between them. The "
"optimization passes do not fuse gates across a barrier, so every rotation "
"survives:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A \\`FermionicCircuit\\` with repeated steps separated by "
"barriers.](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-fermions/circuit-12.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Note\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  The `reps` argument of [`FermionicSuzukiTrotter`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-library-synthesis-fermionic-suzuki-trotter#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis.FermionicSuzukiTrotter \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis.FermionicSuzukiTrotter\") is a different concept. It subdivides a single [`Evolution`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-library-evolution#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.Evolution \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.Evolution\") gate into more, shorter factors, whereas [`repeat()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.repeat \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.repeat\") duplicates whole circuits. Repeating a circuit that holds one [`Evolution`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-library-evolution#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.Evolution \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.Evolution\") only multiplies its total evolution time.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Measure fermionic modes {#measure-fermionic-modes}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[`measure_all()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-"
"fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.measure_all "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.measure_all\") reads every mode "
"out into a classical bit of its own, pairing mode `i` with bit `i`. Use "
"[`measure()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-"
"fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.measure "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.measure\") with your own "
"[`ClassicalRegister`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/circuit#qiskit.circuit.ClassicalRegister)"
" to read out only some of them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Passing `inplace=False` returns a measured copy and leaves the original "
"alone, which matters because a measurement is not unitary: a circuit "
"carrying one can no longer be applied to a state vector, so keep an "
"unmeasured circuit around if you also want to simulate it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Measuring here, rather than on the transpiled "
"[`QuantumCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.circuit.QuantumCircuit),"
" is what keeps the sampled bitstrings easy to read. A fermionic measurement "
"names the mode it reads, so every stage that moves modes around "
"([`RelabelModes`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/transpiler-passes-relabel-"
"modes#qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.RelabelModes "
"\"qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.RelabelModes\") at the fermionic level, "
"layout and routing at the qubit level) carries the measurement along with "
"it, and the bitstring stays indexed by the mode you asked about.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Note\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Unlike [`measure_all()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.circuit.QuantumCircuit#measure_all), no [`barrier()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.barrier \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.barrier\") is inserted before the measurements. Call [`barrier()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.barrier \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.barrier\") yourself first if you want one there.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Whether a measured bit means what you expect depends on the fermion-to-qubit encoding, which is chosen during transpilation rather than here. Under an occupation-basis encoding such as Jordan-Wigner each mode is carried by one qubit and the bit is that mode’s occupation; see [`FermionicMeasure`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-library-fermionic-measure#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.FermionicMeasure \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.FermionicMeasure\").\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Transpile fermionic circuits {#transpile-fermionic-circuits}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To implement the quantum algorithm represented by your "
"[`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") it must be transpiled to a "
"[`QuantumCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.circuit.QuantumCircuit)."
" You can learn how to do this in the [Transpile fermionic "
"circuits](transpilation#transpilation-explanation) guide.\n"
msgstr ""

